WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.570
Malcolm Grant: Let us move on, then, to the
next item on the agenda: item four. This is

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:12.920
the Commissioning Round Update. I am going
to invite Barbara Hakin to introduce this.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:19.920
Barbara Hakin: Thank you, Chairman. As everyone
will be aware, the planning and contracting

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:26.490
round that takes place at this time of year
is really key and critical for patients and

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:32.770
for ensuring the right services are available
for them. I tend to see commissioning, in

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:39.109
its simplest form, as three broad parcels
of activity: planning, agreeing and monitoring.

00:00:39.109 --> 00:00:44.839
Actually, this particular time of year is
when we focus, really, on the planning.

00:00:44.839 --> 00:00:50.749
For the micro planning of services we work
with patients, stakeholders and other communities

00:00:50.749 --> 00:00:56.889
to understand the shape of services, the clinical
quality of services and how to make services

00:00:56.889 --> 00:01:00.409
sensitive to patient's needs. That goes on
throughout the year. However, at this time

00:01:00.409 --> 00:01:07.409
of the year we bring together the macro planning.
This aims to identify exactly how much capacity,

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:13.909
in terms of staff, beds or equipment, the
NHS needs to delver services for patients.

00:01:13.909 --> 00:01:20.229
We also agree, between commissioners and providers,
the contracts which will underpin that.

00:01:20.229 --> 00:01:25.299
This process is therefore vital for our providers.
From the macro planning they need to understand

00:01:25.299 --> 00:01:29.750
what they need to put in place so they can
have the capacity to deliver the services

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:35.520
for patients. Furthermore, it is vital for
us as commissioners because it needs to give

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:41.179
us confidence that we know the things that
are important for patients will be delivered.

00:01:41.179 --> 00:01:48.179
For example, it is important to us that we
are able to plan for the number of outpatients

00:01:48.929 --> 00:01:52.049
that are needed, to serve patients' needs.
We also need to be able to plan for the number

00:01:52.049 --> 00:01:56.359
of patients who are likely to turn up in our
A&amp;E department, as Simon has just described.

00:01:56.359 --> 00:02:00.729
We also must determine the number of patients
that will need planned surgical operations.

00:02:00.729 --> 00:02:07.729
We must ensure that there is sufficient activity
within the plans we make with the hospitals

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:15.010
that our patients can be treated in a timely
manner and we will not slip behind. The really

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:22.010
important targets that have been set for us
were set because we know patients find waiting

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:30.840
for a diagnosis or treatment difficult. Therefore,
that planning part is really important.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:34.950
Of course, the agreeing part comes in here
because it is where the contracting happens.

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:40.580
Furthermore, if we get the plans right, it
sets us the basis for monitoring for the future.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:44.310
That is therefore why I think this paper is
so important. I think the Board should recognise

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:48.160
the work that has gone into that and the progress
we have made.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:55.160
The paper is in several parts. The first part
talks about the CCG position. It is our assessment

00:02:56.710 --> 00:03:03.710
of CCG plans. Work on that continues. For
the most part, CCG plans are agreed with their

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:11.880
local providers. The contracts are set. Furthermore,
in our first run of the things that we look

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:18.880
at, we are confident that CCGs are planning
for the right amount of activity, compared

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:25.710
to previous trends. As Simon said, we have
seen some changes in acute activity. Therefore,

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:28.730
we will have to be vigilant about that as
the year goes on.

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:33.400
However, we do not only look at that. We look
at many other things we know are important.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:39.000
Things that we, as NHS England, have agreed
that we will deliver as part of the mandate

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:44.380
and on which we are dependent with CCGs and
their providers for delivery. For example,

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:51.110
we are looking very closely with CCGs at the
plans for dementia diagnosis which are coming

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:56.470
through. It is an important issue for us.
How much access to psychological therapies

00:03:56.470 --> 00:04:02.280
do we consider is being commissioned? Are
other aspects of parity of esteem being delivered?

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:08.910
Is there a good increase in services for mental
health? This is in addition to a range of

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:11.990
other things. That requires many iterations.

00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:18.990
We have recently received the second, much
more robust, cut of CCG plans. It shows us

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:23.270
the macro level activity plans are in place
and we now need to go round with them again.

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:28.290
This is especially the case for those plans
where we think CCGs are either not planning

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:32.100
for something that is correct, or where we
think they are perhaps not being particularly

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:36.950
ambitious. I think, certainly in some of the
mental health areas, we would want to hold

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:42.630
up a mirror to them and ask them whether their
ambitions were appropriate.

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:49.630
The next part of the paper describes what
Simon has already described: putting in place

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:56.540
additional money to support both winter pressures
and elective activities. It describes for

00:04:56.540 --> 00:05:00.870
the Board how we have strengthened what were
once known as the urgent care working groups.

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:05.820
It appeared evident that they needed to look
at resilience around elective care, not only

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:12.070
resilience for urgent care. I would therefore
like the Board to note the change of name.

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:16.389
I would also like to note, not only the new
moneys that Simon mentioned, but also the

00:05:16.389 --> 00:05:20.280
guidance we are putting in place. This guidance
is to support the System Resilience Groups

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:25.740
in the very important job they will have to
do as we move into the winter. They must make

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:30.930
sure that, on their local patch, everything
is being done to ensure that patients can

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:36.030
be treated quickly and efficiently, whether
they need urgent care or planned care.

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:41.770
The next section of the paper goes on to describe
what is happening in specialised commissioning.

00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:45.610
We all know specialised commissioning has
been extremely difficult. I think that in

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:50.150
this next year we will still be struggling
on the back of many of the issues that came

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:56.410
into play in our first year. These include
problems with budgets and problems with activity.

00:05:56.410 --> 00:06:02.450
The paper describes a turnaround team we have
put in place. I asked Paul Watson to take

00:06:02.450 --> 00:06:08.530
on responsibility for a turnaround team to
improve where we were. As you see, the paper

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:15.000
shows that at one stage we were showing an
£800 million problem in specialised commissioning.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:22.000
Simon ensured that turnaround team was properly
resourced and brought together. One of the

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:28.950
issues for specialised was that the people
working in it were split across the organisation.

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:34.800
We brought extra resource into that team:
senior, high level resource. We also co ordinated

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:37.169
better and this, together with the resource,
has made a huge difference to our ability

00:06:37.169 --> 00:06:38.950
to operate.

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:44.110
I would really like to say a special thanks
to Paul. I would also like to thank the others

00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:48.400
who have come into that turnaround team. Paul
has managed to do this as well as his Regional

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:52.290
Director job. Many of the others have also
been seconded from important jobs they were

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:57.050
doing and people had to backfill around them.
Therefore, thank you for all of that.

00:06:57.050 --> 00:07:04.050
You will see from the table that we have now,
largely, got a balanced plan for specialised

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:11.560
commissioning. The table in 20 was a position
at the time the paper was written. We have

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:18.690
now resolved the issues around the £65 million
deficit plan in London. However, I would really

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:24.820
like to bring the Board's attention to the
risks in some of these plans. This will still

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:30.130
be very difficult and very challenging. Our
area Team Directors are responsible for ensuring

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:34.490
this happens. They will need to work very
hard and be very vigilant. However, there

00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:41.389
are some risks which are out of their control.
The table does identify some of the big risks

00:07:41.389 --> 00:07:47.710
we will have to watch in specialised, as we
watch risks for all the areas for which we

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:49.300
are responsible.

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:56.300
I will mention that you will see that we have,
for the most part, signed off all contracts

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:02.680
for specialised commissioning. We have hundreds
of contracts for specialised commissioning.

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:08.090
The vast majority are resolved. For a very
small number there is a problem of either

00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:12.270
hundreds of thousands or in and around the
million. However, with one trust we have a

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:19.270
difficulty of a £75 million gap between ourselves
and the trust on that contract. That will

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:23.990
need to be resolved.

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:29.669
I will not dwell on the final section because
Simon has done the Better Care Fund. The details

00:08:29.669 --> 00:08:36.669
are in the paper. Mine is a cross government
role, as the SRO for this. Again, there is

00:08:38.139 --> 00:08:42.809
a great deal of work to be done on this. I
think the Better Care Fund presents an enormous

00:08:42.809 --> 00:08:47.410
opportunity to do things differently. It is
a huge chance for health and social care,

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:52.519
particularly out of hospital, to work together
to do things differently. They could help

00:08:52.519 --> 00:08:56.490
make sure patients who do not need to be in
hospital do not have to be and can be looked

00:08:56.490 --> 00:09:03.149
after at home. However, I do think the programme
itself needs, very swiftly, some rigorous

00:09:03.149 --> 00:09:07.600
programme governance arrangements and programme
management arrangements put in place. I think

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:12.480
we need to go out to the service. We need
to go out to local authorities, through the

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:15.499
health and wellbeing Boards of our CCGs and
look for much more rigour in their plans.

00:09:15.499 --> 00:09:19.399
They need to plan exactly what they intend
to do and how this money will be spent. We

00:09:19.399 --> 00:09:24.949
need to ask where the evidence is that what
they are going to do is going to improve services

00:09:24.949 --> 00:09:31.949
for patients and also reduce pressures on
social care, long term and our acute trusts.

00:09:32.790 --> 00:09:35.279
Therefore, I think there is a lot to do there.

00:09:35.279 --> 00:09:40.290
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Barbara.
Let me invite questions or comments from colleagues

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:43.149
around the table. Moira?

00:09:43.149 --> 00:09:49.720
Moira Gibb: I am probably not the only person
trying to guess which hospital that is. Is

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:55.410
it University College Hospital in London?
Could you say a little bit more about how

00:09:55.410 --> 00:10:02.410
likely that is to get resolved? It is a significant
outlier, shall we say?

00:10:03.619 --> 00:10:10.619
Barbara Hakin: It is University College Hospital,
London. You are absolutely right. I think

00:10:10.699 --> 00:10:16.860
a £75 million gap is a very large gap and
it is unusual that we have such a large gap

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:23.860
with one institution when the vast majority
of other things are being resolved. The most

00:10:23.860 --> 00:10:29.209
senior members of the London regional team
continue to work with University College Hospital,

00:10:29.209 --> 00:10:36.209
London to try and resolve this. There are
a range of issues. However, they are for the

00:10:37.379 --> 00:10:42.540
most part the same issues which have been
dealt with in other hospitals, such as large

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:48.029
specialist providers across London. Some of
the disagreements are about the growth in

00:10:48.029 --> 00:10:55.029
activity. Again, we want to understand where
there is an assumption that the growth in

00:10:55.699 --> 00:11:01.059
activity there would be different. There is
a significant QIPP ambition in that. Furthermore,

00:11:01.059 --> 00:11:05.679
there are some issues about some business
cases that they have and how we use CQUIN.

00:11:05.679 --> 00:11:12.220
However, again, the CQUIN agreement has been
reached with other organisations.

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:17.740
There is an issue with Project Diamond which
is probably common to other organisations

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:19.379
and should not just be seen as part of that.

00:11:19.379 --> 00:11:21.240
Malcolm Grant: I think you might just need
to explain what Project Diamond is.

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:24.509
Barbara Hakin: Right. Okay. I am very sorry,
I should have done.

00:11:24.509 --> 00:11:31.509
In the past there has been government, central
funding for a number of very, very highly

00:11:32.980 --> 00:11:39.980
specialised cases. At the moment there is
disagreement between parties as to how that

00:11:41.059 --> 00:11:44.769
funding should flow. The disagreement is about
whether it is wrapped up in the current contracts

00:11:44.769 --> 00:11:47.100
or whether it should be additional.

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:54.100
I think it would be only fair to say to University
College that that difference of opinion exists

00:11:54.829 --> 00:12:00.899
with several hospitals, not just them. However,
they have actually shown it as part of this

00:12:00.899 --> 00:12:07.769
deficit. We do need to continue to ensure
that the plans for this are absolutely right

00:12:07.769 --> 00:12:14.769
and the financial flows are right. For that
community of patients, the more that is spent

00:12:16.329 --> 00:12:21.059
on this, the less there is available to spend
on the frontline community services, health

00:12:21.059 --> 00:12:25.559
improvement services and primary care services
which we know that part of London so desperately

00:12:25.559 --> 00:12:26.069
needs.

00:12:26.069 --> 00:12:27.579
Malcolm Grant: Margaret?

00:12:27.579 --> 00:12:31.720
Margaret Casely Hayford: That response was
actually very helpful. From that response

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:38.129
it sounds as though the issues that University
College are highlighting are very much the

00:12:38.129 --> 00:12:45.129
same issues as other hospitals. In that regard,
I have to say I am really quite concerned.

00:12:45.139 --> 00:12:52.139
I have a great deal of disquiet, really. Unless
there is some really, significant issue they

00:12:52.619 --> 00:12:59.619
really do need to focus very carefully on
the huge discrepancy between themselves and

00:13:01.329 --> 00:13:01.730
the others.

00:13:01.730 --> 00:13:05.899
Barbara Hakin: Yes. However, obviously they
are not here. I am sure that they would feel

00:13:05.899 --> 00:13:11.199
their concerns are legitimate. One of the
things I can say is that both teams, theirs

00:13:11.199 --> 00:13:16.769
and ours, are putting a lot of time in to
try and resolve this issue. However, it is

00:13:16.769 --> 00:13:22.110
big. You are absolutely right, we need to
be concerned about this. It just dwarfs everything

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:24.489
else that is left to resolve.

00:13:24.489 --> 00:13:31.029
Malcolm Grant: I should add that the chairman
of UCH has written to me and set out the various

00:13:31.029 --> 00:13:37.679
items which they see as still remaining unresolved
and the reasons for them. However, given the

00:13:37.679 --> 00:13:43.439
concern that is being expressed around the
Board, we need to urge the executive team

00:13:43.439 --> 00:13:49.449
here to make all progress to closing these
negotiations. I think it is disquieting that

00:13:49.449 --> 00:13:55.970
there should be a £75 million outlier. As
I understand it, the nearest other is in hundreds

00:13:55.970 --> 00:13:56.749
of thousands of pounds.

00:13:56.749 --> 00:14:00.059
Barbara Hakin: I think it is slightly over
£1 million, in fairness. However, it is that

00:14:00.059 --> 00:14:00.709
sort of order.

00:14:00.709 --> 00:14:05.980
Malcolm Grant: Can you just help us? What
happens if this continues to remain unresolved?

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:12.980
Barbara Hakin: In the past there have been
contracts which have been unresolved for quite

00:14:13.199 --> 00:14:20.149
a few months through the year. This is probably
one of those which, in the past, has been

00:14:20.149 --> 00:14:27.149
unresolved through the year. How each component
is dealt with is slightly different. Whilst

00:14:29.819 --> 00:14:36.819
we want to get the plans right, we will have
to pay for activity if the patients need to

00:14:37.029 --> 00:14:44.029
be treated. That therefore resolves. That
path is called payment by results. When we

00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:48.850
pay for activity that has happened at a hospital,
that is called payment by results. Some of

00:14:48.850 --> 00:14:55.329
the other areas are outside that. If we cannot
resolve it, for those we would move through

00:14:55.329 --> 00:14:57.119
an arbitration process.

00:14:57.119 --> 00:15:00.040
Malcolm Grant: Okay. Victor?

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:07.040
Victor Adebowale: If the negotiations continue,
we are paying for the activity anyway? Obviously

00:15:08.029 --> 00:15:15.029
we do not want to carry on the negotiations
here. However, it would appear that if this

00:15:15.749 --> 00:15:22.749
goes on, we are effectively paying the £75
million. Is that right or have I misunderstood

00:15:25.230 --> 00:15:25.759
something fundamental?

00:15:25.759 --> 00:15:31.299
Barbara Hakin: No, it is not. When we agree
plans we are agreeing broad plans. It is really

00:15:31.299 --> 00:15:35.089
important to get that as near to right as
possible because a lot falls out of that.

00:15:35.089 --> 00:15:40.850
However, the payment by results system does
mean that, as commissioners, for patients

00:15:40.850 --> 00:15:45.299
who are referred and need to be treated, we
make the payment. If we have the plans wrong,

00:15:45.299 --> 00:15:55.039
we will pay extra at the end. This is why
it is such a risk to us as a specialised commissioner,

00:15:55.089 --> 00:15:58.399
particularly with these very high cost services.
In the final analysis, we have to pay for

00:15:58.399 --> 00:15:59.259
the patients who are treating.

00:15:59.259 --> 00:16:04.059
So, no, we are not paying for something we
would not otherwise have paid for. However,

00:16:04.059 --> 00:16:11.059
that is not to say that there are some agreements
in the margins of that which could affect

00:16:12.149 --> 00:16:13.869
exactly how that activity is understood.

00:16:13.869 --> 00:16:15.040
Victor Adebowale: Okay.

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:16.600
Malcolm Grant: Okay. Ciaran?

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:22.949
Ciaran Devane: Can I ask a broader question?
Assuming that gets resolved, we are broadly

00:16:22.949 --> 00:16:29.389
in balance, lots of plans in place, is it
all doable? Or is it that all things have

00:16:29.389 --> 00:16:32.920
to perfect in all places at all times?

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:39.769
Barbara Hakin: I would say, having been in
the NHS for a lot of years and I turn to Paul

00:16:39.769 --> 00:16:45.239
as well, that this has been the most difficult
year. Probably somebody will say we say that

00:16:45.239 --> 00:16:48.929
every year. However, no, I would put my hand
on my heart and say this is the most difficult

00:16:48.929 --> 00:16:55.929
year we have faced, in terms of balancing
the plans. I am particularly concerned about

00:16:56.109 --> 00:17:01.749
the specialised commissioning plans. I think
even more so than the CCG plans. I am particularly

00:17:01.749 --> 00:17:07.839
concerned about specialised commissioning.
I do think that some of the risks in there

00:17:07.839 --> 00:17:14.839
are very significant indeed. The QIPP ambition
for specialised is considerable and there

00:17:17.860 --> 00:17:21.649
are still some other things that need to go
on. We are in discussions with CCGs and hospitals

00:17:21.649 --> 00:17:28.179
to resolve issues. I think what I would feel
is that, on balance, we have upsides and downsides.

00:17:28.179 --> 00:17:35.179
Paul would want to add to this, I am sure.
However, I think there will be CCGs, in particular,

00:17:39.010 --> 00:17:43.620
whose surplus increases because their risks
are not quite as great as they thought. There

00:17:43.620 --> 00:17:48.679
will also be CCGs whose risks end up greater
than they thought. The same will happen to

00:17:48.679 --> 00:17:53.250
different areas of the country for specialised
and some of our other direct commissioning.

00:17:53.250 --> 00:17:58.389
I think that we feel, as an executive team,
that taking these plans as a whole, with the

00:17:58.389 --> 00:18:00.169
upsides and the downsides of risk, it is doable,
yes.

00:18:00.169 --> 00:18:05.529
Malcolm Grant: And that is something to which
we will return in the next paper.

00:18:05.529 --> 00:18:06.809
Barbara Hakin: Sorry, yes.

00:18:06.809 --> 00:18:10.950
Malcolm Grant: Right. Can we thank you for
that? Although, could I actually just ask

00:18:10.950 --> 00:18:15.389
one question, which is around Cancer Drugs
Fund. You mentioned in the paper that a new

00:18:15.389 --> 00:18:19.149
approach to it was being developed and this
is another potential area of risk for us.

00:18:19.149 --> 00:18:20.549
Could you say a few more words about that?

00:18:20.549 --> 00:18:25.789
Barbara Hakin: Yes. There is no doubt that
the Cancer Drugs Fund represents a significant

00:18:25.789 --> 00:18:32.789
risk to us because there are more drugs becoming
available that could potentially benefit patients.

00:18:34.380 --> 00:18:39.980
Some of the drugs are now looking as if they
are not as useful as we first thought they

00:18:39.980 --> 00:18:45.519
might be. However, there is significant risk
in the Cancer Drugs Fund and we are working

00:18:45.519 --> 00:18:49.470
with a variety of stakeholders to identify
how we can mitigate that risk.

00:18:49.470 --> 00:18:55.940
Malcolm Grant: Okay. Thank you, Barbara. I
think we receive the report. We note the exposure

00:18:55.940 --> 00:19:02.730
to risk across all of the areas you have identified
here. As a Board, I think we will want to

00:19:02.730 --> 00:19:06.580
watch this very carefully. However, perhaps
we could wrap that up into the next paper

00:19:06.580 --> 00:19:12.029
